course: biology
lesson: 202
title: Hamsters, gerbils and pet rats and mice
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: A hamster can stuff its cheeks so full of seeds that its head looks twice as wide, because stretchy pouches run all the way back to its shoulders, like two shopping bags.
explain: In the wild a hamster carries that food home to its burrow at night, so a pet hamster sleeps all day and runs on its wheel while the family sleeps.
explain: Gerbils come from deserts, so they dig tunnels, drink very little and thump their back feet to warn each other, while pet rats are clever enough to learn their names and even laugh when tickled.
explain: Cartoons show Jerry the mouse stealing cheese, but real mice prefer seeds, grain and sweet things, and every small pet needs daily care, not just a cage in a corner.
fact: A hamster's cheek pouches stretch back to its shoulders, and it can carry a big load of food in them, some say up to half its body weight, to store in its burrow.
fact: Hamsters are active at night; in the wild they run many kilometres looking for food, so a pet needs a big solid wheel that keeps its back straight.
fact: A hamster woken suddenly is frightened and may bite, so it is best to let it wake up and come to you in the evening.
fact: Syrian hamsters live alone in the wild and attack other hamsters, so they must be kept one per cage, while some dwarf hamsters can live in pairs.
fact: Rodent front teeth have a hard orange layer and keep growing all their life, so yellow-orange teeth are normal and healthy.
fact: Gerbils come from deserts where they live in tunnels, so they love a deep layer of bedding to dig in.
fact: Gerbils drum their back feet to warn the colony of danger or when excited, and they are social, so they should live in pairs or groups.
fact: Gerbils save water by making very dry droppings and very little urine (wee), so they drink little and their cages smell less than many others.
fact: Pet rats are clever and friendly: they learn tricks and their names, and they chirp when tickled, a kind of laugh too high for people to hear without a machine.
fact: Mice do not especially love cheese; they prefer seeds, grains and sweet foods, and the cheese idea may come from old times when cheese was stored where mice could reach it.
fact: Small pets need clean bedding, fresh water, the right food and things to do every day, and hamsters live only about two to three years.
fact: Mice, rats, hamsters and gerbils all walk on four legs; hamsters have a very short tail, while mice, rats and gerbils have long ones.
exercise: How far back do a hamster's cheek pouches reach? | To its shoulders | Only to its teeth | To its tail | To its ears
exercise: Why does a wild hamster fill its cheeks with seeds? | To carry food back to its burrow to store | To make its face look big and scary to foxes and owls | To keep its teeth warm | To feed other animals on the way
exercise: Why does a hamster run on its wheel all night? | Hamsters are active at night and run far for food in the wild | It is trying to escape from the bad dreams it has in the daytime | It only runs when the lights are on | The wheel makes it sleepy
exercise: What kind of wheel does a hamster need? | A big solid one that keeps its back straight | A tiny one with thin gaps between the bars so it can grip with its claws | No wheel at all, ever | A wheel made of soft paper
exercise: Why might a hamster bite a child who woke it up? | It was frightened by being woken suddenly | It wanted to say good morning | It thought the finger was cheese | Hamsters bite everyone at all times
exercise: What happens if two grown Syrian hamsters are put in one cage? | They fight | They become best friends | They sleep all day together | They share their food happily
exercise: A hamster's front teeth are yellow-orange. What does this mean? | This is normal and healthy for a rodent | It ate too many carrots | It needs its teeth brushed with a tiny toothbrush every night | It has a bad tooth illness
exercise: Why do gerbils dig deep tunnels in their tank? | They come from deserts where they live in tunnels | They are trying to dig all the way down to water deep under the sand | They want to bury their owner's toys | They dig only when they are ill
exercise: Why does a gerbil drum its back feet? | To warn others of danger or because it is excited | Because its feet are cold and drumming warms them up | To dance to music | Because it wants to be alone
exercise: Why does a gerbil drink very little water? | It is a desert animal that saves water | It is afraid of water | It gets all the water it needs from the air it breathes | It drinks only once a year
exercise: What can a pet rat learn? | Its name, and to come when called | To speak a few English words, like a parrot | To read its name on the cage | Nothing, rats cannot learn
exercise: What do rats do when tickled? | Chirp in a laugh too high for us to hear | Cry like a baby | Bite and run away every time, because they hate being touched | Fall asleep at once
exercise: What do mice really prefer to eat? | Seeds, grains and sweet foods | Holey yellow cheese above all | Only meat | Only leaves
exercise: Where may the idea of mice loving cheese come from? | Cheese was once stored where mice could reach it | Mice cannot smell anything but cheese, so it is all they ever find | Scientists proved it long ago | Cheese is the only food mice can chew
exercise: How long does a pet hamster usually live? | About two to three years | About twenty years | About two months | About ten years
exercise: Which pet must be kept alone in its cage? | A Syrian hamster | A gerbil | A pet rat | A guinea pig
test: In Tom and Jerry, Jerry carries a huge wedge of cheese. If you had a real pet mouse, which treat would it like more? | A few seeds or a bit of grain | A big slab of hard cheese | A bowl of salty crisps | A cup of milk
test: «3 gerbils and a hamster in the room, how many legs altogether» | 16 | 12 | 20 | 14
test: «got 2 Syrian hamsters for my bday, put em together so they're not lonely» What is the best answer? | Keep them apart, Syrian hamsters fight | Good idea, they love company | Put a third one in too | Only keep them together at night
test: «oi why's my hamster's face all fat, is he sick or what» What is the most likely reason? | He has stuffed his cheek pouches with food | He has a toothache from eating too much cheese in one go | He is holding his breath | He has eaten his wheel
test: «gerbil cage smells way less than the rabbit one, weird» Why? | Gerbils make dry droppings and very little urine | Gerbils never go to the toilet | Gerbils clean the cage themselves every night with their paws and tails | Gerbils smell like flowers
test: What would happen if a child woke a hamster at noon every day to play? | The hamster would be stressed and more likely to bite | The hamster would become a day animal at once | The hamster would grow faster | Nothing, hamsters like noon best
test: «my mum says hamsters are easy, just a cage and food, done» What is right? | They need clean bedding, water, food and things to do every day | Yes, they need nothing else | They only need feeding once a month, because they store food in their cheeks | They clean and feed themselves
test: A girl's pet rat comes running when she calls «Pip!». What does this show? | Rats are clever and can learn their names | Rats can understand every word she says, like a person | It was a lucky accident | Rats only come for cheese
test: «Rat teeth orange, gross, should I whiten them» What is right? | No, orange front teeth are normal and healthy | Yes, rats need white teeth to stay healthy and strong | Yes, they are rotting | No, you should pull them out
test: In a cartoon a hamster has a long fluffy tail like a squirrel. What does a real hamster have? | A very short tail | A long bare tail | Two tails | A fluffy tail like a fox
test: A gerbil thumps its feet hard and the others run into their tunnels. What did the thump mean? | A warning of danger | An invitation to eat | A sign it is going to sleep | A song for the owner
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the housing of Syrian hamsters? | Adult Syrian hamsters ought to be housed singly, since they attack others of their kind | They ought invariably to be housed in groups of six or more | They require the company of a gerbil | They may be housed with any rodent whatsoever
exam: Identify the principal reason for which the gerbil consumes comparatively little water. | It is adapted to desert conditions and conserves water by producing dry droppings and little urine | It obtains all water from cheese | It is incapable of swallowing liquids, its throat being too narrow for any drink | It drinks exclusively during hibernation
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the diet of mice. | Mice prefer seeds, grains and sweet foods, and cheese is not their favourite | Mice subsist entirely upon cheese, which they locate by its odour at great distances | Mice are strictly carnivorous | Mice consume nothing but leaves
exam: It is given that the front teeth of a hamster are of a yellow-orange colour. Ascertain the correct interpretation. | The colour is normal, since rodent incisors bear a hard orange layer | The colour denotes a grave disease | The colour results from excessive consumption of carrots | The colour indicates that the teeth have ceased growing
